    "updated_at": "2018-01-29T13:33:21Z",
    "body": "So we have HTML's \"the body element\", but we also have a CSS definition which is different (because it doesn't care about frameset).\r\n\r\nThe HTML definition is used in HTML (normatively that has JS-exposed behavior) by\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-tree-accessors:the-body-element-2-3 (document.body getter and setter)\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focus-processing-model:the-body-element-2-2 (focus processing model for key events?)\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focus-management-apis:the-body-element-2 (document.activeElement)\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#drag-and-drop-processing-model:the-body-element-2 (drag and drop)\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#other-elements,-attributes-and-apis:the-body-element-2-2 (`document.fgColor` et al)\r\n\r\nThe CSS definition is used by\r\nhttps://quirks.spec.whatwg.org/#the-body-element-fills-the-html-element-quirk\r\nhttps://quirks.spec.whatwg.org/#the-tables-inherit-color-from-body-quirk\r\nhttps://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#dom-document-scrollingelement + more scrolling APIs\r\nhttps://drafts.csswg.org/css2/colors.html#background\r\nhttps://drafts.csswg.org/css2/visufx.html#propdef-overflow\r\n(and probably some more things?)\r\n\r\nWhat CSS does is probably out of scope for this issue but worth noting if the interest is making things consistent.\r\n\r\nI see these options\r\n\r\n* Firefox/Edge change document.body to be like the spec. Seems like the shortest path to interop, but the behavior is inconsistent between getter/setter/CSS. (Also Safari/Edge still needs to fix `document.bgColor` et al to be per spec.)\r\n* Chrome/Safari/Edge change document.body setter to throw like Firefox. Behavior is more consistent between getter/setter, still inconsistent with CSS.\r\n* Chrome/Safari/Firefox change document.body getter and setter to be like Edge. Behavior is more consistent between getter/setter, still inconsistent with CSS.\r\n* Something else to make everything more consistent.\r\n\r\nAny thoughts?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
